WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.720
Speaker 1: Tax the rich is a popular slogan&nbsp;
among the left. Taxing the wealthiest is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.760
quite well supported by Americans across&nbsp;
political parties, although certainly less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.200
so by Republicans, although tax the rich is&nbsp;
relatively popular overall. But taxing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.000
rich is actually difficult if what we're talking&nbsp;
about is collecting taxes from the rich. And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.160
I mean by that is many of the methods proposed&nbsp;
to tax the rich either wouldn't really tax the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.400
rich because the rich would find ways around&nbsp;
them, or they would be completely impractical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.080
So I'll explain. You raise income taxes. You&nbsp;
say we're going to raise that top tax rate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:45.360
on income from 36 to 39 to 42 to 50 to 60. OK.&nbsp;
Most of the very rich aren't earning their money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.000
through income through wages, so they're not&nbsp;
actually going to be impacted by an increase to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.280
the top income tax rate. They make a lot of money&nbsp;
through capital gains. OK, well, so then you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:04.160
Well, let's raise capital gains. The rich making&nbsp;
money from dividends and making money from money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.920
will have to pay if we increase the capital gains&nbsp;
tax rate or tax capital gains as we do income. OK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.600
Well, there's a way around that as well. The rich&nbsp;
can choose not to realize the capital gains. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.800
avoid those taxes. They can wait around for the&nbsp;
next president or the one after that to lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.320
the tax rate on capital gains. Or they can create&nbsp;
charitable foundations, which they control or use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.240
other mechanisms. OK, well, you've got the estate&nbsp;
tax for people who die with more than a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.160
dollars or 22 million for couples. There we've&nbsp;
got them, right? Well, not really, because you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.480
reduce tax liability there as well with all sorts&nbsp;
of other mechanisms, including stepped up basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:52.160
Delaware dynasty trusts. I could go on and on as&nbsp;
a business owner, you can reduce taxable income by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.240
contributing to a defined benefit plan and then&nbsp;
you earn income from the plan, paying taxes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.600
income later when your income may be lower, but&nbsp;
reducing income taxes in the immediate. OK, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.840
hold on. So then let's do a wealth tax. Let's&nbsp;
tax people's assets, even unrealized gains on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.560
those assets. I've already explained why I think&nbsp;
it's untenable, and some of you disagree with me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.480
many times. For the very wealthy net worth is tied&nbsp;
up, either in their business and physical assets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:31.280
in accounts receivable. In stocks with unrealized&nbsp;
gains, you may not actually have the liquid assets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:38.960
to cover a wealth tax. And then imagine that&nbsp;
you pay such a tax on stocks you haven't sold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:46.080
And then next year, the market crashes and you&nbsp;
lose value in those stocks. Are you entitled to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:53.360
refund on the net worth tax that you paid? As I've&nbsp;
said before, I believe it's completely unfeasible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.200
I think there's essentially no chance that what&nbsp;
Elizabeth Warren is proposing on a wealth tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.360
passes. And part of what I'm getting to here&nbsp;
is something called the dynamic tax model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.800
When you do a tax model and you score it, how&nbsp;
much will we collect? You really should do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.880
dynamically, meaning you account for how people's&nbsp;
behavior will change based on the tax plan. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.800
I'll give you an example. If right now we say,&nbsp;
listen, if you look at people's income right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:32.880
if we were to raise the top marginal tax rate&nbsp;
from 37 percent to 45 percent, we'd collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:40.640
$200 billion. I'm just making these numbers up.&nbsp;
That only holds if we assume nobody changes their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:48.480
behavior. But the truth is, if you announced&nbsp;
the tax is changing from 37 to 45 percent, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:56.640
top tax rate many people subject to the top tax&nbsp;
rate would reduce their income. Take it in stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.000
deferred compensation, move personal expenses to&nbsp;
business expenses, etc. So you need to score these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.600
proposals dynamically to account for changes in&nbsp;
behavior. And when you do that, you realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.840
the actual government revenue change from&nbsp;
a lot of these proposals is much smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.000
than what we might have immediately imagined.&nbsp;
OK, great. So these are all the problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.400
How do we actually collect taxes from the rich?&nbsp;
I actually don't know, because just about every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.680
individual change that I've read about can at&nbsp;
least be partially defeated, somewhat mitigated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.960
And even if not, people always can abandon&nbsp;
the U.S. or whatever country has the tax and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.840
go elsewhere. Now, it's not that easy to do in&nbsp;
the United States. You may have to renounce your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.800
citizenship in the United States to avoid taxation&nbsp;
in the United States if you're a citizen. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.040
let's talk about what might actually&nbsp;
work. My conclusion after studying this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.840
for a very long time is if you really&nbsp;
want to collect taxes from the rich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:04.720
you and other countries need to basically take&nbsp;
all of the above approach to make it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:11.680
difficult to avoid taxation. It's not even going&nbsp;
to be impossible, but you've got to start calling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.800
people's bluff about leaving the country, for&nbsp;
example. I think that's what it would take. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.680
you've got to raise the top marginal tax rate on&nbsp;
income. You've got to tax capital gains as income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.520
You've got to eliminate the stepped up basis for&nbsp;
the estate tax. I've talked about it before. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.840
don't I'm not going to explain it in this segment,&nbsp;
but there I have segments on it. You've got to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.680
in a more aggressive alternative minimum tax for&nbsp;
higher earners. You've got to do it all together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.280
and say, Listen, if you really want to avoid&nbsp;
this stuff, you'll have to leave. And maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.560
other countries will do the same thing, by the&nbsp;
way. So then where where are you leaving to?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:51.200
And most people aren't actually going to abandon&nbsp;
their country in this case of the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.320
and you're going to be able to pick up some taxes&nbsp;
from all of these different things. I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:03.360
there's really any other way to reliably collect&nbsp;
taxes from the rich. I understand passing policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:10.080
that targets the rich, but actually collecting&nbsp;
is the difficult part. If you have a better idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.040
let me know, but I think that that's what&nbsp;
it would take. Reminder, we are six days&nbsp;&nbsp;

